n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\). Index the rows and columns of the grid by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nA unit square is identified with the ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) where \\(i\\) is the row and \\(j\\) the column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reformulation as a matrix covering problem  \n\nPlace a 0 in the entry \\((i,j)\\) exactly when the square \\((i,j)\\) is left uncovered; all other entries receive a 1.  \nBecause each row and each column must contain precisely one uncovered square, the set of 0‑entries is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}=0\\iff j=\\sigma(i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle; in matrix language a tile corresponds to a submatrix\n\\[\nA\\times B\\;=\\;\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in A,\\;j\\in B\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(B\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of consecutive indices.  \nThe tile may be placed only if the submatrix contains no 0‑entry, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(A\\times B)\\cap\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem asks for the smallest number of such submatrices that together contain **all** 1‑entries of \\(M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound via a fool‑off set  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,i)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll elements of \\(S\\) are 1‑entries because the only 0‑entries are the pairs \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nTake any two distinct elements of \\(S\\).  \nIf they are \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,j)\\) with \\(i,j\\ge 2\\), then the “cross’’ entry \\((i,j)\\) equals 0 because, after a suitable renaming of rows and columns, we may assume \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)=j\\); consequently \\(i=j\\) forces \\((i,j)\\) to be a 0‑entry.  \n\nHence **no rectangle satisfying (2)