e ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) where \\(i\\) is the row and \\(j\\) the column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reformulation as a matrix covering problem  \n\nPlace a \\(0\\) in the entry \\((i,j)\\) exactly when the square \\((i,j)\\) is left uncovered; all other entries receive a \\(1\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain precisely one uncovered square, the set of \\(0\\)-entries is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nM_{ij}=0\\iff j=\\sigma(i). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis\u2011aligned rectangle; in matrix language a tile corresponds to a submatrix\n\\[\nA\\times B=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in A,\\;j\\in B\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(B\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of consecutive indices.  \nThe tile may be placed only if the submatrix contains no \\(0\\)-entry, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(A\\times B)\\cap\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\varnothing . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem asks for the smallest number of such submatrices that together contain **all** \\(1\\)-entries of \\(M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound via a fool\u2011off set  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(1,i)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll elements of \\(S\\) are \\(1\\)-entries because the only \\(0\\)-entries are the pairs \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n\nTake any two distinct elements of \\(S\\).  \nIf they are \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,j)\\) with \\(i,j\\ge 2\\), the smallest axis\u2011aligned rectangle containing them consists of the rows \\(\\{1,\\dots ,i\\}\\) and the columns \\(\\{1,\\dots ,j\\}\\).  \nThis rectangle necessarily contains the diagonal square \\((k,k)\\) where \\(k=\\min\\{i,j\\}\\); that square is a \\(0\\)-entry by (1).  \nConsequently **no rectangle satisfying (2) can contain two different elements of \\(S\\)**.\n\nSince each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(S\\), any covering of all \\(1\\)-entries must use at least  \n\n\\[\n|S|=2(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  This gives a universal lower bound, independent of the permutation \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  An explicit covering with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nPermuting rows and columns does not affect the minimal number of rectangles needed: if a family of rectangles covers the matrix for a given permutation, applying the same permutation to the rows and columns of the family yields a covering for the original matrix with the same cardinality.  \nTherefore we may assume, without loss of generality, that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal ones:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the **horizontal** rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) consists of the squares in row \\(i\\) that lie strictly to the right of the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\); it is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle and contains only \\(1\\)-entries.\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the **vertical** rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) consists of the squares in column \\(j\\) that lie strictly below the diagonal square \\((j,j)\\); it is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle and also contains only \\(1\\)-entries.\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, because any square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs either to a unique horizontal rectangle (if \\(j>i\\)) or to a unique vertical rectangle (if \\(i>j\\)), but never to both.  \n\nMoreover, every off\u2011diagonal square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly one of these rectangles:\n* if \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\);\n* if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).\n\nThus the union of the \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\}\\) covers **all** \\(1\\)-entries of the matrix, while avoiding every diagonal \\(0\\)-entry.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Optimality  \n\nEquation (3) shows that no covering can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\#\\text{tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
