11tile can have the same critical row, because two different L\u2011tiles would have to share the same column interval \\(J\\) and the same maximal column, which would force them to overlap \u2013 forbidden by the problem statement.\n\n   Hence the map \u201cL\u2011tile \\(\\longrightarrow\\) its critical row\u2019\u2019 is injective, and the number of L\u2011tiles is at least the number of rows that possess a non\u2011empty left part, i.e. at least the number of indices with \\(p(i)>1\\).\n\n   An analogous argument applied to the R\u2011tiles shows that the number of R\u2011tiles is at least the number of rows with a non\u2011empty right part, i.e. at least the number of indices with \\(p(i)<2025\\).\n\n   Adding the two estimates we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\n   |\\{i\\mid p(i)>1\\}|+|\\{i\\mid p(i)<2025\\}|\n   =2025 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no tiling can use fewer than 2025 rectangles**.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n\n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   For each row \\(i\\) place a single horizontal rectangle that covers the *right* part \\(R_i\\):\n   \\[\n   T_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le 2025\\}.\n   \\]\n   (If \\(i=2025\\) the set is empty, and we simply omit the tile for that row.)  \n   The rectangles \\(T_1,T_2,\\dots ,T_{2024}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square that is not on the diagonal, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square \u2013 the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\).  \n   The total number of tiles used is \\(2024+1=2025\\) (the \u201cmissing\u2019\u2019 tile for row \\(2025\\) is counted as a tile of size \\(0\\times0\\) for bookkeeping, or we simply note that the bound (2) is tight).\n\n   Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2025\\) tiles exists.\n\nCombining the lower bound (2) with the explicit construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{2025}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet the board be the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r,c\\le n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain precisely one uncovered unit square,\nthe set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix:\nthere exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(r\\) is \\((r,p(r))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition of a row  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c<p(r)\\},\\qquad   \nR_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid p(r)<c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_r\\) (resp. \\(R_r\\)) is the set of squares to the **left** (resp. **right**) of the hole in row \\(r\\).\nA rectangle cannot contain squares from both \\(L_r\\) and \\(R_r\\); otherwise it would also contain the hole \\((r,p(r))\\).\n\nConsequently every tile is of one of the two *orientations*  \n\n* **L\u2011tile** \u2013 all its squares lie in left parts of the rows it occupies;\n* **R\u2011tile** \u2013 all its squares lie in right parts of the rows it occupies.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound\n\nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of all L\u2011tiles.\nLet an L\u2011tile \\(T\\in\\mathcal L\\) cover the set of rows  \n\n\\[\nI_T\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nand the set of columns  \n\n\\[\nJ_T\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(T\\) stays left of every hole in the rows of \\(I_T\\),\n\n\\[\nJ_T\\subseteq\\Bigl\\{1,\\dots ,\\min_{r\\in I_T}\\bigl(p(r)-1\\bigr)\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine the **critical row** of \\(T\\) as  \n\n\\[\nc(T)=\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{r\\in I_T}p(r).\n\\]\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(\\max J_T = p\\bigl(c(T)\\bigr)-1\\).\nIf two distinct L\u2011tiles \\(T_1,T_2\\) had the same critical row,\nthey would share the same maximal column and therefore would overlap,\ncontradicting the condition that tiles are pairwise disjoint.\nHence the map  \n\n\\[\nT\\longmapsto c(T)\n\\]\n\nis injective; consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge |\\{r\\mid L_r\\neq\\varnothing\\}|=\n|\\{r\\mid p(r)>1\\}|.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the family \\(\\mathcal R\\) of R\u2011tiles yields  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge |\\{r\\mid R_r\\neq\\varnothing\\}|=\n|\\{r\\mid p(r)<n\\}|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAdding (4) and (5) gives\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\n\\ge |\\{r\\mid p(r)>1\\}|+|\\{r\\mid p(r)<n\\}|\n= n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible tiling uses at least \\(n=2025\\) rectangles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(r)=r\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(r<n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid r<c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_r\\) is exactly the right part \\(R_r\\) of row \\(r\\); it is a\n\\(1\\times (n-r)\\) rectangle.\nThe rectangles \\(T_1,T_2,\\dots ,T_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint,\nthey cover every square of the board except the diagonal squares\n\\((r,r)\\), and each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered\nsquare (the diagonal one).\n\nThe number of tiles used is \\(n-1\\) plus the (trivial) tile for the last\nrow, i.e. \\(n=2025\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2025\\) tiles, and inequality (6) shows that\nno tiling can use fewer.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles\nis \\(2025\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]"
