ight of holes. The rectangle's column interval includes columns from p(r)+1 to p(k)+1 (assuming p(r) < p(k)). That interval does not include p(r). So hole (r,p(r)) is not covered. However, does the rectangle also contain any other hole? Possibly (k,p(k))? The column interval includes p(k) only if p(k) is between p(r)+1 and p(k)+1 inclusive? Actually p(k) is less than p(k)+1, so column p(k) is not included unless p(k) >= p(r)+1. Since p(k) > p(r), p(k) >= p(r)+1. So column p(k) is in the interval [p(r)+1, p(k)+1]? Let's check: The interval includes columns p(r)+1, p(r)+2, ..., p(k)+1. p(k) is between p(r)+1 and p(k) (since p(k) > p(r)). So p(k) is in the interval. So the rectangle would contain column p(k). For row k, the rectangle includes row k, column p(k). So the tile would contain hole (k,p(k)). Indeed, because the rectangle includes row k and column p(k). So the tile would contain the hole (k,p(k)). So the tile cannot cover both s_r and s_k if both s_r and s_k are to the right of holes. Let's check: The rectangle includes rows from r to k (or k to r). For row k, column p(k) is included because column interval includes p(k). So the tile would contain hole (k,p(k)). So indeed covering two s_r squares on the right side leads to covering a hole for the later row. So the lemma's argument about column p(r) might be mis-specified but the conclusion that the tile cannot cover two s_r squares is correct. Let's verify more carefully.\n\nSuppose p(r) < p(k) and both holes are not at column n. Then s_r = (r,p(r)+1), s_k = (k,p(k)+1). The rectangle covering both must include rows from r to k inclusive and columns from p(r)+1 to p(k)+1 inclusive. For row k, column p(k) is in the column interval because p(k) ∈ [p(r)+1, p(k)+1] (since p(k) ≥ p(r)+1). So the rectangle includes (k,p(k)), which is the hole for row k. So tile would contain a hole, contradiction. So indeed T_r ≠ T_k.\n\nNow consider the case where one of the holes is at column n, so s_k = (k,n-1) (left side). Suppose p(r) < n (so s_r = (r,p(r)+1)). Then tile covering s_r and s_k must include rows from r to k and columns from min(p(r)+1, n-1) to max(p(r)+1, n-1). Since n-1 > p(r)+1 (if p(r)+1 ≤ n-1). So column interval includes columns from p(r)+1 up to n-1. Does it include column p(k) = n? No, because column n is not in interval. However, hole (k,n) is at column n, not covered. So maybe tile covering s_r and s_k does not contain hole (k,n). But does it contain hole (r,p(r))? Column interval includes p(r)+1 to n-1, not p(r). So hole (r,p(r)) not covered. So tile covering s_r and s_k might be allowed? Let's test with a concrete example: n=5. Suppose holes at (r=1, p(1)=3) and (k=2, p(2)=5). So s_1 = (1,4), s_2 = (2,4) because p(2)=5 => s_2 = (2,4) (n-1). The rectangle covering (1,4) and (2,4) is just column 4 rows 1-2, which is a 2×1 rectangle. Does this rectangle contain any hole? It contains (1,4) and (2,4). Holes are at (1,3) and (2,5). Neither is covered. So this tile covers two s_r squares without covering a hole. So the map r → T_r is not injective in this case. So the lower bound argument fails for rows where one hole is at the rightmost column.\n\nThus the lower bound of n may not hold. However, the solution's construction uses n tiles. Could there be a construction with fewer than n tiles? Possibly yes, using some rows where holes are at column n, allowing a tile to cover multiple s_r squares. So the lower bound is not proven correctly. So the answer may be less than n.\n\nThus the solution's answer may be wrong (i.e., not minimal). Let's try to find a better construction for n=2025. The problem asks for minimum number of tiles. The solution claims n = 2025. But perhaps we can do with fewer tiles, maybe around ceil(n/2) or something. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover all squares except a permutation matrix. The uncovered squares are exactly one per row and column. So we need to choose a permutation p. The goal is to cover the rest with as few rectangles as possible.\n\nObservation: If we choose a permutation where many holes are at the rightmost column (or leftmost column), then the squares to the right of those holes are minimal (or left side minimal). This may allow covering multiple adjacent squares with a single rectangle.\n\nAlternatively, we could choose a permutation where holes are all in the same column? Not allowed because each column must have exactly one hole. So we need a permutation.\n\nOne approach: Choose p such that holes are placed in a monotone decreasing order (like p(i) = n+1-i). Then the holes are on the anti-diagonal. The left side of each row is columns 1 to p(i)-1, which is many squares for early rows (i small). The right side is columns p(i)+1 to n. For i small, p(i) is large, so right side is small. For i large, p(i) is small, left side is small. This might allow grouping left side squares into vertical rectangles.\n\nSpecifically, consider p(i) = n+1-i (reverse diagonal). Then holes are at (i, n+1-i). For each row i, left side L_i = columns 1 to n-i (since p(i)-1 = n-i). Right side R_i = columns n+2-i to n (if p(i) < n). For i=1, p(1)=n, left side columns 1..n-1, right side empty (since p(1)=n). For i=n, p(n)=1, left side empty, right side columns 2..n. So the left side squares for rows 1..n-1 form a shape: row i has left side columns 1..n-i. This shape is a staircase shape (upper-left triangle). Similarly, the right side squares for rows 2..n form a lower-right triangle.\n\nWe could cover the left side triangle with n-1 rectangles? Actually we could cover the left side triangle with n-1 vertical rectangles: For each column j from 1 to n-1, the left side includes squares (i,j) for rows i ≤ n-j. So column j has squares from row 1 to row n-j. That's a vertical rectangle of size (n-j)×1. So we can cover each column j with a vertical rectangle. That's n-1 rectangles. Similarly, the right side triangle can be covered with n-1 horizontal rectangles. But we need to avoid covering holes. However, each column 