                            "text": "We need to evaluate the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution provided:\n\n- Defines B = set of coordinates, n = 2025.\n- Since each row and column must have exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection p: {1,...,n} → {1,...,n} such that the unique uncovered square in row r is (r, p(r)).\n- Defines L_r = squares left of the hole, R_r = squares right of the hole.\n- Argues that a rectangle cannot contain squares from both L_r and R_r, because then it would also contain the hole. So each tile is either L-tile (all squares in left parts of rows it occupies) or R-tile (all squares in right parts).\n- Lower bound: For each row r, define s_r = adjacent square to the hole: (r, p(r)+1) if p(r) < n, else (r, n-1) if p(r) = n. This square is covered. Let T_r be the unique tile covering s_r. Lemma: map r → T_r is injective. Proof: assume T_r = T_k for r≠k. WLOG p(r) < p(k). Then tile contains squares (r,p(r)+1) and (k,p(k)+1). Since tile's column set is an interval, it must contain column p(r). Then tile would contain hole (r,p(r)), contradiction. So distinct rows give distinct tiles. So at least n tiles needed.\n- Construction: Choose p(r) = r (main diagonal). For each row r < n, place horizontal rectangle T_r = { (r,c) | r < c ≤ n } = R_r. So T_r is a 1 × (n - r) rectangle covering the right part of row r. The rectangles T_1,...,T_{n-1} are pairwise disjoint; they cover all squares except diagonal squares (r,r). Each row and column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal). Number of tiles used: n-1 plus \"trivial empty tile for the last row\", i.e., exactly n.\n- Minimality: lower bound n, construction achieves n, so answer is n = 2025.\n\nWe need to evaluate according to the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, identify problem type: It asks for minimum number of tiles. That's an optimization problem (minimum). So Level 1: For optimization problems, we do not check answer correctness at this level (we cannot know ground truth). But we need to check for arithmetic errors or constraint violations. The solution claims answer is 2025. We need to see if any arithmetic errors or constraints violation. The lower bound argument seems plausible; we need to verify if any hidden errors exist. The solution uses a lemma that each row's adjacent square must be covered by a distinct tile. That seems plausible. But we need to check if any hidden assumption is wrong: Are we sure that each tile's column set is an interval of consecutive columns? Since tiles are rectangles, yes: any rectangle's set of columns is an interval. Similarly rows are intervals.\n\nBut we need to verify that the tile covering s_r is unique: each square is covered by at most one tile, so indeed each s_r is covered by exactly one tile (since it's covered). So T_r is defined.\n\nThe lemma: If T_r = T_k, then tile contains squares (r,p(r)+1) and (k,p(k)+1). The tile's column set is an interval, so it contains all columns between p(r)+1 and p(k)+1 inclusive. But they claim it must contain column p(r). Wait, we need to check: Suppose p(r) < p(k). Then p(r)+1 ≤ p(k)+1? Actually p(r)+1 ≤ p(k)+1 because p(r) < p(k). So the interval of columns covered by the tile includes all columns from p(r)+1 to p(k)+1 inclusive. Does that guarantee column p(r) is included? The interval includes columns p(r)+1, p(r)+2, ..., p(k)+1. Column p(r) is one less than p(r)+1, so it's not necessarily included. So the claim that the tile must contain column p(r) is questionable. Let's examine: The tile covers squares (r,p(r)+1) and (k,p(k)+1). Since a rectangle covers a contiguous block of rows and columns, the rows covered must be a contiguous interval containing rows r and k. The columns covered must be a contiguous interval containing columns p(r)+1 and p(k)+1. The column interval is [c_min, c_max] where c_min ≤ p(r)+1 and c_max ≥ p(k)+1. Actually the interval must include both p(r)+1 and p(k)+1, so c_min ≤ min(p(r)+1, p(k)+1) = p(r)+1, and c_max ≥ max(p(r)+1, p(k)+1) = p(k)+1. So the column interval includes all columns from p(r)+1 up to p(k)+1. It does not include column p(r) unless p(r)+1 = p(r) which is impossible. So the argument that the tile must contain column p(r) is flawed. However, perhaps they intended to consider the row r's left side L_r and right side R_r. They argued that a tile cannot contain squares from both left and right of the hole in a given row. But the tile covering s_r is on the right side of the hole for row r (since s_r is the square adjacent to the hole on the right, or if hole is at column n, they pick column n-1 which is left side). So T_r is an R-tile (or L-tile if hole at n). The lemma tries to show that T_r cannot be same as T_k because otherwise the tile would contain a hole. But the argument as given is flawed: they claim that the tile would contain column p(r) because column interval includes p(r). But column interval includes p(r)+1 to p(k)+1, not p(r). So we need to examine if there is another reason why T_r cannot equal T_k. Let's think: Suppose we have two rows r and k, with holes at (r,p(r)) and (k,p(k)). Suppose p(r) < p(k). The tile covering s_r = (r,p(r)+1) also covers (k,p(k)+1). Since tile is a rectangle, it must also cover all squares with rows between r and k (inclusive) and columns between p(r)+1 and p(k)+1 (inclusive). In particular, it covers (r,p(r)+1) (by definition) and (r,p(r)+2) up to (r,p(k)+1). It also covers (k,p(k)+1) and (k,p(k))? Wait, column p(k) is one less than p(k)+1, which is not guaranteed to be in the column interval. So maybe the tile does not cover (r,p(r)). However, does it cover the hole (r,p(r))? The hole is at column p(r). The tile's column interval is from p(r)+1 to p(k)+1. So column p(r) is not covered. So the tile does not contain the hole (r,p(r)). So the argument that tile would contain the hole is not correct. So the lemma fails.\n\nThus the lower bound argument may be flawed. However, maybe we can salvage it: If p(r) = n, they define s_r = (r,n-1) which is left of the hole. In that case, the tile covering s_r is an L-tile. If we have two rows r and k with holes at p(r) = n and p(k) < n, then s_r = (r,n-1) is left side, s_k = (k,p(k)+1) is right side. If they are covered by same tile, then the tile must contain both left side of row r and right side of row k. But a tile cannot contain squares from both left and right of a hole in a given row, but here the holes are in different rows. So that may be okay. So the lemma's argument fails.\n\nThus the lower bound of n tiles may be invalid. Let's think: Could we tile with fewer than n tiles? Let's attempt to find a construction with fewer than n tiles. The solution's construction uses n tiles: n-1 horizontal rectangles covering right side of each row, plus a trivial empty tile for last row. But maybe we can tile with n-1 tiles? Let's think: We need each row and each column to have exactly one uncovered square. So we need a set of n uncovered squares forming a permutation matrix. The uncovered squares are exactly one per row and column. The tiles cover all other squares, at most one tile per square, but can be any shape rectangles. We need to minimize number of rectangles covering all other squares. This is akin to covering all squares except a permutation matrix with rectangles that cannot include any of those uncovered squares. The uncovered squares are like \"holes\". The problem is to cover the rest of the board with rectangles that avoid the holes. The lower bound may be at least n because each row's uncovered square splits the row into left and right parts, and each tile cannot cross the hole. So each tile must be entirely on one side of the hole in each row it occupies. So each tile can be thought of as covering a region that is either left of all holes in the rows it occupies, or right of all holes. But holes may be at different columns per row, so the region might be irregular. The lower bound might be more than n.\n\nLet's analyze the problem more thoroughly: We have a grid of size n×n (n=2025). We need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned rectangles covering unit squares) covering all squares except exactly one per row and column. Equivalent to covering all squares except a permutation matrix.\n\nGoal: minimize number of rectangles.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all squares except a permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution suggests answer is n (2025). But is it minimal? Let's think about known results: This is reminiscent of covering a board with rectangles avoiding a set of points (holes). The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all cells except a set of n cells (forming a permutation) might be n. But is that always minimal? Let's consider small n.\n\nTest small n manually (though we cannot manually test as per instruction, but we can reason). Let's consider n=2. The board is 2×2. Need each row and column to have exactly one uncovered square. So the uncovered squares must be a permutation: either (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's try to cover the other squares with rectangles. For (1,1) and (2,2) uncovered, the covered squares are (1,2) and (2,1). These are not adjacent in a rectangle shape (they are not contiguous horizontally or vertically). They are two separate squares. Each rectangle must be axis-aligned and cover a set of unit squares forming a rectangle. The two squares are not in the same row or column, so you cannot cover both with a single rectangle (since rectangle covering both would also include (1,1) or (2,2)). Actually a rectangle covering (1,2) and (2,1) would be the whole 2×2 board, which includes holes. So you need two rectangles: one covering (1,2) and one covering (2,1). So you need 2 rectangles, which equals n. So for n=2, answer is 2.\n\nFor n=3: Let's try to see if we can cover with 3 rectangles. The uncovered squares must be a permutation matrix: e.g., (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). The covered squares are all other 6 squares. Can we cover them with 3 rectangles? Possibly yes: For each row i, cover the right side of the hole: row 1: squares (1,2),(1,3) as a 1×2 rectangle; row 2: squares (2,3) as a 1×1 rectangle; row 3: no squares to right (hole at column 3). So we need 2 rectangles? Actually row 1: rectangle covering (1,2)-(1,3) (size 1×2). Row 2: rectangle covering (2,3) (size 1×1). Row 3: no rectangle needed. That's 2 rectangles, not 3. But we need each column also have exactly one uncovered square. In this arrangement, column 1 has uncovered (1,1); column 2 has uncovered (2,2)? Wait column 2 uncovered is (2,2) which is not in column 2? Actually (2,2) is column 2. So column 2 has uncovered (2,2). Column 3 uncovered (3,3). So each column has exactly one uncovered square. The covered squares: (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (2,3), (3,1), (3,2). The rectangle covering (1,2)-(1,3) covers (1,2),(1,3). The rectangle covering (2,3) covers (2,3). But we still need to cover (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). Could we cover them with one rectangle? Let's see: (2,1) and (3,1) are in column 1, rows 2 and 3. That's a rectangle of size 2×1 covering (2,1),(3,1). That rectangle does not include any holes because holes are at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). So we can have rectangle covering (2,1)-(3,1). Then we need to cover (3,2). That's a single square; could be a 1×1 rectangle. So total rectangles: (1,2)-(1,3) (size 1×2), (2,3) (1×1), (2,1)-(3,1) (2×1), (3,2) (1×1). That's 4 rectangles. Could we do better? Maybe we can use a rectangle covering (2,1)-(3,2) which is a 2×2 rectangle covering (2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(3,2). But (2,2) is a hole, so not allowed. So can't.\n\nAlternatively, we could choose a different permutation: perhaps (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Let's try to see if we can cover with 3 rectangles. The holes are at (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). The covered squares are all others. Could we cover them with 3 rectangles? Let's attempt: Row 1: squares left of hole: (1,1). Row 1: squares right of hole: (1,3). Row 2: left: (2,1),(2,2). Row 2: right: none. Row 3: left: none. Row 3: right: (3,2),(3,3). So we have left side squares: (1,1),(2,1),(2,2). Right side squares: (1,3),(3,2),(3,3). Could we cover left side squares with a rectangle? They are at columns 1 and 2 across rows 1 and 2. The rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes (1,2) which is a hole. So cannot. Could we cover left side squares with two rectangles: (1,1) alone, and (2,1)-(2,2) as a 1×2 rectangle. Right side squares: (1,3) alone, (3,2)-(3,3) as a 1×2 rectangle. That's total 4 rectangles. So maybe minimal is 4 for n=3? But we need to check if we can do with 3. Let's try a different arrangement: maybe we can use a rectangle covering (1,1)-(3,1) (3×1) covering column 1 except hole at (3,1) is a hole, so not allowed. So cannot. So maybe minimal is 4 for n=3. But the solution claims minimal is n=3, which would be 3. So we need to test if we can achieve 3 rectangles for n=3. Let's attempt to find a construction with 3 rectangles covering all squares except a permutation.\n\nWe need to cover 9-3=6 squares with 3 rectangles, average rectangle size 2 squares. So each rectangle must cover exactly 2 squares on average. Could be 2+2+2 or 3+2+1 etc. Let's try to find a covering with 3 rectangles.\n\nOne approach: Use a rectangle covering (1,1)-(2,2) (2×2) covering squares (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). But (1,2) or (2,2) might be holes. If we choose holes such that they are not inside that rectangle. For example, choose holes at (1,3),(2,3),(3,1). Then the rectangle (1,1)-(2,2) does not contain any holes (holes are at column 3 for rows 1 and 2, and at row 3 column 1). So rectangle (1,1)-(2,2) covers 4 squares. Then we need to cover remaining squares: (1,3) is a hole, (2,3) is a hole, (3,2),(3,3) are uncovered squares? Actually holes are (1,3),(2,3),(3,1). The uncovered squares are holes; we need to cover all other squares: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3). The rectangle (1,1)-(2,2) covers (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). The remaining squares are (3,2),(3,3). Those can be covered by a rectangle (3,2)-(3,3) (1×2). That's a second rectangle. So we have 2 rectangles total, not 3. Wait we need to cover all squares except holes, but we have covered all non-hole squares with 2 rectangles. So we used 2 rectangles, which is less than n=3. But is this allowed? Let's check constraints: Each tile must be a rectangle with sides on grid lines, covering unit squares, no overlap, each unit square covered at most once. That's fine. The holes are (1,3),(2,3),(3,1). Are there any uncovered squares besides holes? No. So we have a covering with 2 rectangles: rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 (size 2×2) and rectangle covering row 3, columns 2-3 (size 1×2). That's valid. Does each row have exactly one uncovered square? Row 1: uncovered square is (1,3) (hole). Row 2: uncovered square is (2,3). Row 3: uncovered square is (3,1). Indeed each row has exactly one uncovered square. Each column: column 1: uncovered square is (3,1). Column 2: uncovered squares? Wait column 2 has no holes: holes are at columns 3,3,1. So column 2 has no uncovered squares. That's a problem: The condition requires each column also has exactly one uncovered square. In our arrangement, column 2 has no uncovered square. So this arrangement fails the column condition. So we need a permutation: each column must have exactly one hole. So the holes must be a permutation matrix: each column appears exactly once. So the holes must be (r,p(r)) for a bijection p. In our example, holes are at (1,3),(2,3),(3,1). This is not a permutation because column 3 appears twice. So invalid. So we must have a permutation.\n\nThus the holes must be a permutation matrix. For n=3, the permutations are: identity (1,1),(2,2),(3,3); (1,1),(2,3),(3,2); (1,2),(2,1),(3,3); (1,2),(2,3),(3,1); (1,3),(2,1),(3,2); (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). There are 6 permutations.\n\nWe need to find a covering with fewer than 3 rectangles for any of these permutations. Let's test each quickly conceptually.\n\nPermutation identity: holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Covered squares: all others. Can we cover all 6 squares with 2 rectangles? Possibly: rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 2-3? That would be a 3×2 rectangle covering columns 2 and 3 across rows 1-3. This rectangle includes squares (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3). But (2,2) and (3,3) are holes, so not allowed. So can't.\n\nMaybe rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 2-3 (2×2) covering (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). Contains hole (2,2). Not allowed.\n\nMaybe rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 (2×2) covering (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). Contains holes (1,1) and (2,2). Not allowed.\n\nThus any rectangle covering more than one row and column will likely include a hole. So perhaps we need to use rectangles that are either horizontal strips to the right of each hole or vertical strips to the left of each hole, etc. The solution's construction uses n rectangles (n-1 horizontal strips + empty tile). For n=3, that would be 3 rectangles: row 1: right part (1,2)-(1,3) (size 1×2), row 2: right part (2,3) (size 1×1), row 3: empty tile. That's 2 rectangles? Actually they count n-1 + empty tile = 3. So they get 3 rectangles.\n\nBut can we do with 2 rectangles? Let's attempt to find a covering with 2 rectangles for n=3 that satisfies column condition. The holes must be a permutation. Let's try permutation (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). So holes at (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Covered squares: (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3). Can we cover these with 2 rectangles? Let's see: rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 (2×2) includes (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). Contains hole (1,2). So not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-3, column 3 (3×1) covering (1,3),(2,3),(3,3). Contains hole (2,3). Not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-3, column 1 (3×1) covering (1,1),(2,1),(3,1). Contains hole (3,1). Not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 2-3 (2×2) covering (2,2),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3). Contains hole (2,3). Not allowed.\n\nConsider rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 2-3 (3×2) includes holes (1,2) and (2,3). Not allowed.\n\nThus any rectangle covering more than one row or column seems to include a hole. So maybe we need to use rectangles that are either horizontal strips to the right of holes (like solution) or vertical strips to left of holes. The solution uses n rectangles. Could we reduce number by using some vertical strips that cover multiple rows? For example, suppose we have holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). The left side of each row is empty for row 1 (since hole at column 1), left side of row 2 is column 1, left side of row 3 is columns 1-2. Could we cover left side squares with a vertical rectangle? For rows 2 and 3, left side includes column 1 for row 2 and columns 1-2 for row 3. Not a rectangle because row 2 left side is column 1 only, row 3 left side includes column 2 as well. So cannot combine.\n\nBut maybe we can use a rectangle covering rows 2-3, column 1 (2×1) covering (2,1),(3,1). That rectangle does not include holes (2,2) or (3,3). So that's allowed. Then we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,3),(3,2). Could we cover those with a rectangle? Possibly rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 2-3 (3×2) includes holes (2,2) and (3,3). Not allowed. Could we cover (1,2)-(1,3) as a horizontal rectangle (1×2), and (2,3)-(3,3) as vertical rectangle (2×1) but (3,3) is a hole. So not allowed.\n\nThus we might need at least 3 rectangles. So for n=3, minimal might be 3. The solution's lower bound of n seems plausible for small n.\n\nBut we found a flaw in the lemma. However, the lower bound might still hold by a different argument. Let's examine the lemma more carefully: They defined s_r as the square adjacent to the hole: if hole not at rightmost column, s_r = (r,p(r)+1). If hole at rightmost column, s_r = (r,n-1). So s_r is always to the right of the hole, except when hole at n, they choose left side (n-1). So s_r is always on the side of the hole that is \"right\" except for the rightmost case where they choose left side. The tile covering s_r is T_r. They claim map r → T_r is injective. The proof: assume T_r = T_k for r≠k. WLOG p(r) < p(k). Then T_r contains squares (r,p(r)+1) and (k,p(k)+1). Since tile's column set is an interval, it must contain every column between those two, in particular column p(r). Hence tile would also contain hole (r,p(r)), contradiction.\n\nBut as we argued, column interval includes columns from p(r)+1 up to p(k)+1, not p(r). So the claim is false. However, maybe they intended to consider the case where p(k) = n? No, they defined s_k = (k,n-1) if p(k) = n. Then s_k is left of the hole. So if p(k) = n, then s_k = (k,n-1). Then T_k contains (k,n-1). If p(r) < p(k) = n, then s_r = (r,p(r)+1). So T_r contains (r,p(r)+1) and (k,n-1). The column interval includes columns from p(r)+1 up to n-1. This includes column p(r) if p(r)+1 > p(r)? Actually p(r)+1 > p(r), so column p(r) is not included. So still not.\n\nThus the lemma is flawed. But does the lower bound still hold? Perhaps there is a stronger argument: each tile can cover at most one of the s_r squares because covering two s_r squares would cause a hole to be covered. But we need to check if that is true. Let's examine: Suppose a tile covers s_r and s_k. s_r is either (r,p(r)+1) or (r,n-1). s_k is either (k,p(k)+1) or (k,n-1). The tile is a rectangle covering rows from min(r,k) to max(r,k) and columns from min(c1,c2) to max(c1,c2). If both s_r and s_k are on the right side of their holes (i.e., p(r) < n, p(k) < n), then both are to the right of holes. The rectangle's column interval includes columns from p(r)+1 to p(k)+1 (assuming p(r) < p(k)). That interval does not include p(r). So hole (r,p(r)) is not covered. However, does the rectangle also contain any other hole? Possibly (k,p(k))? The column interval includes p(k) only if p(k) is between p(r)+1 and p(k)+1 inclusive? Actually p(k) is less than p(k)+1, so column p(k) is not included unless p(k) >= p(r)+1. Since p(k) > p(r), p(k) >= p(r)+1. So column p(k) is in the interval [p(r)+1, p(k)+1]? Let's check: The interval includes columns p(r)+1, p(r)+2, ..., p(k)+1. p(k) is between p(r)+1 and p(k) (since p(k) > p(r)). So p(k) is in the interval. So the rectangle would contain column p(k). For row k, the rectangle includes row k, column p(k). So the tile would contain hole (k,p(k)). Indeed, because the rectangle includes row k and column p(k). So the tile would contain the hole (k,p(k)). So the tile cannot cover both s_r and s_k if both s_r and s_k are to the right of holes. Let's check: The rectangle includes rows from r to k (or k to r). For row k, column p(k) is included because column interval includes p(k). So the tile would contain hole (k,p(k)). So indeed covering two s_r squares on the right side leads to covering a hole for the later row. So the lemma's argument about column p(r) might be mis-specified but the conclusion that the tile cannot cover two s_r squares is correct. Let's verify more carefully.\n\nSuppose p(r) < p(k) and both holes are not at column n. Then s_r = (r,p(r)+1), s_k = (k,p(k)+1). The rectangle covering both must include rows from r to k inclusive and columns from p(r)+1 to p(k)+1 inclusive. For row k, column p(k) is in the column interval because p(k) ∈ [p(r)+1, p(k)+1] (since p(k) ≥ p(r)+1). So the rectangle includes (k,p(k)), which is the hole for row k. So tile would contain a hole, contradiction. So indeed T_r ≠ T_k.\n\nNow consider the case where one of the holes is at column n, so s_k = (k,n-1) (left side). Suppose p(r) < n (so s_r = (r,p(r)+1)). Then tile covering s_r and s_k must include rows from r to k and columns from min(p(r)+1, n-1) to max(p(r)+1, n-1). Since n-1 > p(r)+1 (if p(r)+1 ≤ n-1). So column interval includes columns from p(r)+1 up to n-1. Does it include column p(k) = n? No, because column n is not in interval. However, hole (k,n) is at column n, not covered. So maybe tile covering s_r and s_k does not contain hole (k,n). But does it contain hole (r,p(r))? Column interval includes p(r)+1 to n-1, not p(r). So hole (r,p(r)) not covered. So tile covering s_r and s_k might be allowed? Let's test with a concrete example: n=5. Suppose holes at (r=1, p(1)=3) and (k=2, p(2)=5). So s_1 = (1,4), s_2 = (2,4) because p(2)=5 => s_2 = (2,4) (n-1). The rectangle covering (1,4) and (2,4) is just column 4 rows 1-2, which is a 2×1 rectangle. Does this rectangle contain any hole? It contains (1,4) and (2,4). Holes are at (1,3) and (2,5). Neither is covered. So this tile covers two s_r squares without covering a hole. So the map r → T_r is not injective in this case. So the lower bound argument fails for rows where one hole is at the rightmost column.\n\nThus the lower bound of n may not hold. However, the solution's construction uses n tiles. Could there be a construction with fewer than n tiles? Possibly yes, using some rows where holes are at column n, allowing a tile to cover multiple s_r squares. So the lower bound is not proven correctly. So the answer may be less than n.\n\nThus the solution's answer may be wrong (i.e., not minimal). Let's try to find a better construction for n=2025. The problem asks for minimum number of tiles. The solution claims n = 2025. But perhaps we can do with fewer tiles, maybe around ceil(n/2) or something. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to cover all squares except a permutation matrix. The uncovered squares are exactly one per row and column. So we need to choose a permutation p. The goal is to cover the rest with as few rectangles as possible