1\\) and therefore would miss the squares \\((j,c_i),\\dots ,(j,c_j-1)\\) that belong to the left block of row \\(j\\).  \nHence a single left‑aligned tile cannot serve the left blocks of two rows whose uncovered columns are distinct.\n\nSince the values \\(\\{c_i\\mid i\\in L\\}\\) are precisely the numbers \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (the column \\(1\\) occurs only for the unique row \\(i_0\\) with \\(c_{i_0}=1\\)), we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of left‑aligned tiles }\\ge |\\,\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\,| = n-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAn entirely symmetric argument applied to the right side of each row yields the same conclusion for **right‑aligned** tiles.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{\\,i\\mid c_i<n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in R\\) the squares \\((i,c_i+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) must be covered by a right‑aligned tile whose leftmost column is at least \\(c_i+1\\).  \nTwo rows with different values of \\(c_i\\) cannot share the same right‑aligned tile, because the leftmost column would have to be \\(\\ge\\max(c_i,c_j)+1\\) and would then miss part of the right block of the row whose hole is farther to the left.  \nSince the values \\(\\{c_i\\mid i\\in R\\}\\) are exactly \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of right‑aligned tiles }\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery tile is either left‑aligned, right‑aligned, or interior (touches neither the left nor the right border).  \nAn interior tile can intersect at most one row: if it intersected two rows \\(i<j\\), its column interval would have to avoid both uncovered squares \\((i,c_i)\\) and \\((j,c_j)\\); being bounded on both sides, this would force the interval to lie entirely either left of \\(\\min(c_i,c_j)\\) or right of \\(\\max(c_i,c_j)\\), contradicting the fact that it does not touch the board’s left or right edge.  \nThus interior tiles contribute at most one to the total count per row, and the inequalities (1)–(2) already dominate any possible savings from interior tiles.\n\nAdding (1) and (2) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells  \n\\[\n(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n\\]\nThese are \\(n-1\\) tiles, each left‑aligned with rightmost column \\(n\\).  \n\n*For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells  \n\\[\n(j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j).\n\\]\nThese are another \\(n-1\\) tiles, each right‑aligned with leftmost column \\(1\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, they cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell).  \nThe total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly this number.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Applying to the given board  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
