o verify that the argument is correct: does each tile intersect at most one unit segment of the path? Let's examine that claim. The path is defined as a polyline connecting uncovered squares via vertical and horizontal unit segments. The path goes from (1,c1) down to (2,c1), then right to (2,c2), down to (3,c2), right to (3,c3), etc. So the path goes through each uncovered square, but the path includes vertical segments at each column c_i from row i to i+1, and horizontal segments at each row i+1 from column c_i to c_{i+1}. The path is a monotone path that goes down and right alternating. The path has 2n-2 unit segments.\n\nNow, the claim: any rectangular tile can intersect at most one unit segment of this path. Reason: If a tile intersected two distinct unit segments, those two segments would be separated by a corner of the path. That corner is exactly one of the uncovered squares (i,c_i). The tile interior would then contain that uncovered square, which is not allowed. Let's examine: The path's corners are at the uncovered squares (i,c_i). Indeed, the path goes from (i,c_i) down to (i+1,c_i) (vertical segment), then right to (i+1,c_{i+1}) (horizontal segment). The corner at (i+1,c_i) is not an uncovered square; the uncovered squares are at (i,c_i) for each i. Actually the corner after the vertical segment is at (i+1,c_i). The path goes down from (i,c_i) to (i+1,c_i). Then it goes right to (i+1,c_{i+1}). So the corner at (i+1,c_i) is a point on the grid line but not necessarily an uncovered square. The uncovered squares are at (i,c_i). The corner after the horizontal segment is at (i+1,c_{i+1}) which is the next uncovered square (since i+1's uncovered square is at (i+1,c_{i+1})). So the path's corners are at (i,c_i) and (i+1,c_i). Actually the path includes both vertical and horizontal segments; the corners are at (i,c_i) (starting point), (i+1,c_i) (the turn), and (i+1,c_{i+1}) (the end of the horizontal segment). So the corner at (i+1,c_i) is not an uncovered squ