y between a and b, not including a or b. So if the vertical segment is at x = i (which is integer), then for the tile interior to contain any point on that segment, we need a < i < b. So the tile's interior must span across that vertical line, meaning the tile includes columns on both sides of that line. The tile would then contain the unit squares on both sides of that line. However, the tile's interior cannot intersect a grid line segment without including the adjacent unit squares. So if a tile intersects a vertical segment, it must contain the unit squares on both sides of that segment. But those unit squares are covered by the tile, which is allowed. However, the uncovered squares are unit squares, not points. So the tile intersecting the vertical segment does not necessarily contain the uncovered square (i,c_i) interiorly. The uncovered square is the unit square at (i,c_i). The vertical segment is the left edge of that square (if we think of coordinates). Actually the left edge of the unit square (i,c_i) is the vertical line at column c_i-1? Wait, we need to be precise about coordinate orientation.\n\nLet's define coordinate system: The grid lines are at integer x coordinates for columns (vertical lines) and integer y coordinates for rows (horizontal lines). The unit square (i,j) could be defined as the set of points (x,y) where i-1 < x < i and j-1 < y < j. Or maybe the opposite: row index corresponds to y coordinate. But anyway, the point (i,j) is at the intersection of grid lines at x=i, y=j. The unit square (i,j) is the region between x in (i-1,i) and y in (j-1,j). So the point (i,j) is the top right corner of the unit square (i,j). The uncovered squares are unit squares (i,c_i). The path goes through points (i,c_i) which are the top right corners of those squares. The vertical segment from (i,c_i) to (i+1,c_i) goes downwards (increasing row index) along the grid line x=i, y from c_i to c_i+1? Actually the vertical segment is at x=i, y from c_i to c_i+1? Wa