revious column c, then fill vertical rectangle covering cells V_i = {(r,j) | 1 ≤ r ≤ i-1, c < j < p(i)} (these are cells to the right of previous frontier and left of new hole). Place one vertical rectangle. If p(i) < c, then fill horizontal rectangle covering cells H_i = {(i,j) | p(i) < j < c} (cells left of previous frontier and right of new hole). Place one horizontal rectangle. Each transition yields one rectangle, total n-1 transitions, each yields one rectangle, so total 2n-2 rectangles (n-1 vertical + n-1 horizontal). All cells covered. So minimal number = 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nThus solution claims answer 4048.\n\nWe need to verify according to the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, identify problem type: It's an optimization problem (minimum number of tiles). So it's a MIN problem. According to Level 1: For optimization problems, we do not check answer correctness at this level; we only check for arithmetic errors and constraint violations. Then proceed to Level 1.5 to check optimality.\n\nThus we must check if the solution has any arithmetic errors or constraint violations. The solution uses n=2025, final answer 2n-2 = 4048. Compute 2*2025 = 4050, minus 2 = 4048. That's correct. No arithmetic errors. Does the solution violate constraints? The solution ensures each tile's sides lie on grid lines (by being axis-aligned rectangles defined by rows/columns). Tiles are disjoint (the construction ensures no overlap). Each row and column have exactly one uncovered cell (by definition of p). So constraints are satisfied.\n\nThus Level 1 passes (no errors). Then Level 1.5: Check optimality.\n\nWe need to test small cases (n=3,4,5) using the proposed construction and see if any alternative yields fewer tiles.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument uses Dilworth's theorem to show at least 2n-2 tiles needed. Is that lower bound correct? We need to see if there is any potential flaw. The argument: Each tile lies entirely in L or R. Then covering L with rectangles is equivalent to covering poset (L, ≤) with chains. Then by Dilworth, min number of chains = width = size of largest antichain. They claim width = n-1. Let's verify that.\n\nDefine L = {(i,j) | j < p(i)}. So for each row i, columns left of the hole. For each column c, there may be multiple rows i where p(i) > c (i.e., hole is to the right of column c). So L includes all cells (i,c) for rows i where p(i) > c.\n\nNow consider antichain: set of cells where no two are comparable under ≤ (i ≤ i' and j ≤ j'). For two cells (i,c) and (i',c'), they are comparable if i ≤ i' and c ≤ c' OR i' ≤ i and c' ≤ c. So they are incomparable if one has larger row and smaller column and the other has smaller row and larger column (i.e., they cross). So an antichain can have at most one cell per column because if two cells share same column c, then whichever has smaller row i ≤ i' yields comparability (since c = c', j ≤ j' holds). So at most one per column. There are n-1 columns that appear in L (all columns except the hole column for each row? Actually each row has a hole column p(i). The set of columns that appear in L across all rows is all columns except those that are holes for all rows? Wait each column is a hole for exactly one row (since p is a permutation). So each column appears as a hole exactly once. So for a given column c, there is exactly one row i0 such that p(i0) = c. For that row, column c is not in L (since j < p(i0) = c, so column c is not left of the hole). For all other rows i ≠ i0, p(i) ≠ c, and if p(i) > c, then column c is in L for that row; if p(i) < c, then column c is not in L for that row. So for each column c, there are n-1 rows where column c may be in L or not. But at least one row (i0) where column c is not in L. So the set of columns that appear in L is all columns except maybe some? Actually each column appears in L for at least one row (since there is at least one row where p(i) > c). For the column that is the maximum column n (largest column), p(i) > n is impossible, but p(i) can be equal to n for exactly one row. For column n, there is no row where p(i) > n, so column n never appears in L. So column n is not in L. Similarly, column 1 appears in L for all rows except the row where p(i) = 1 (since for any other row, p(i) > 1). So column 1 appears many times. So the set of columns that appear in L is all columns except the maximum column n (since no p(i) > n). So there are n-1 columns in L. Good.\n\nThus antichain size ≤ n-1 (since at most one per column). They claim there exists an antichain of size n-1: choose for each column c (c ≠ hole column? Actually each column except the hole column on the right? They say \"for each column c that is not a hole‑column, a row i with p(i)>c (such a row exists because the column n is a hole‑column) yields an antichain of size n-1.\" Let's examine: For each column c that is not a hole column? Actually each column is a hole column exactly once. So they need to pick one cell per column that is not the hole column? But they need to pick a cell (i,c) where p(i) > c. For column c, there is exactly one row where p(i) = c (hole column). For all other rows, p(i) may be > c or < c. They need to find at least one row where p(i) > c. For column n, there is no row with p(i) > n, so they cannot pick a cell for column n. So they pick all columns except n. So antichain size n-1. This seems plausible: choose for each column c from 1 to n-1, pick the row i where p(i) = n (the row whose hole is at column n) if p(i) > c? Actually p(i) = n for exactly one row i0. For that row i0, p(i0) = n > c for all c < n. So for each column c < n, the cell (i0, c) is in L (since c < p(i0) = n). So we can pick all cells (i0, c) for c = 1,..., n-1. But those cells are all in same row i0, so they are comparable because they have same row i0 and columns c1 < c2. So they are comparable (i ≤ i', j ≤ j') holds for c1 < c2. So they form a chain, not an antichain. So that doesn't work.\n\nWe need to find an antichain of size n-1