"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete solution.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch** \u2013  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   * Let \\(n=2025\\).  \n   * For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(c_i\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \n   * Because every column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\pi:i\\mapsto c_i\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n2. **A monotone lattice path.**  \n   * Connect the uncovered squares by the poly\u2011line that, for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\), goes **down** from \\((i,c_i)\\) to \\((i+1,c_i)\\) (a vertical unit segment) and then **right** from \\((i+1,c_i)\\) to \\((i+1,c_{i+1})\\) (a horizontal unit segment).  \n   * The resulting path lies on the grid lines, starts at \\((1,c_1)\\) and ends at \\((n,c_n)\\), and consists of exactly \\(2n-2\\) unit\u2011length segments ( \\(n-1\\) vertical and \\(n-1\\) horizontal).\n\n3. **Interaction of a rectangle with the path.**  \n   * A rectangular tile cannot contain any uncovered square, hence its interior cannot intersect the path.  \n   * Consequently a tile can intersect the path in **at most one** of its unit segments: if a tile intersected two distinct segments, those two segments would be separated by a corner of the path, which is precisely an uncovered square \u2013 impossible.  \n   * Therefore each tile can \u201ccover\u201d at most one segment of the monotone path.\n\n4. **Lower bound.**  \n   * Since the path has \\(2n-2\\) unit segments and each tile can account for at most one of them, any admissible tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\text{minimum number of tiles }\\ge 2n-2 } .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   * Choose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   * For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a **horizontal** tile covering the cells\n     \\[\n     (i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n     \\]\n     These are \\(n-1\\) tiles, each left\u2011aligned with rightmost column \\(n\\).  \n   * For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a **vertical** tile covering the cells\n     \\[\n     (j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j).\n     \\]\n     These are another \\(n-1\\) tiles, each right\u2011aligned with leftmost column \\(1\\).  \n   * The two families are pairwise disjoint, they cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell).  \n   * Total number of tiles used is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * Inequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   * The explicit construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   * Hence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}\n\\]\n\nis the minimum number of rectangular tiles required.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe board consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).  \nMatilda places axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles whose sides lie on the grid lines; the tiles are pairwise interior\u2011disjoint (a unit square is covered by at most one tile).  \nIn each row and each column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the column of the unique uncovered square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \\pi(i)=c_i,\n\\]\n\nis a bijection; i.e. \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A monotone lattice path joining the uncovered squares  \n\nConsider the poly\u2011line \\(P\\) that follows the grid lines in the following order:\n\n\\[\n(1,c_1)\\;\\xrightarrow{\\text{down}}\\;(2,c_1)\\;\\xrightarrow{\\text{right}}\\;(2,c_2)\\;\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{down}}\\;(3,c_2)\\;\\xrightarrow{\\text{right}}\\;(3,c_3)\\;\\dots\\;\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{down}}\\;(n,c_{n-1})\\;\\xrightarrow{\\text{right}}\\;(n,c_n).\n\\]\n\nThus for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) the path contains  \n\n* a **vertical** unit segment from \\((i,c_i)\\) to \\((i+1,c_i)\\), and  \n* a **horizontal** unit segment from \\((i+1,c_i)\\) to \\((i+1,c_{i+1})\\).\n\nConsequently \\(P\\) consists of exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\text{ vertical}+(n-1)\\text{ horizontal}=2n-2\n\\]\n\nunit\u2011length segments, all lying on grid lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  How a rectangle can intersect the path  \n\nA rectangular tile is a closed set whose interior is the Cartesian product of an open interval of rows and an open interval of columns.  \nSince the uncovered squares are precisely the points \\((i,c_i)\\) (the interiors of the unit squares), **no uncovered square may belong to the interior of a tile**.  \n\nIf a tile intersected two distinct unit segments of \\(P\\), those two segments would be separated by a corner of \\(P\\). That corner is exactly one of the uncovered squares \\((i,c_i)\\), which would then lie in the interior of the tile \u2013 a contradiction.  \n\nHence **any tile can intersect at most one unit segment of the path \\(P\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nThe path \\(P\\) contains \\(2n-2\\) unit segments, and each tile can \u201caccount for\u2019\u2019 at most one of them.  \nTherefore any admissible tiling must contain at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles }\\ge 2n-2 } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle covering the cells*\n\n\\[\n(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) rectangles; each one touches the left border of the board and extends to the rightmost column \\(n\\).\n\n*For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle covering the cells*\n\n\\[\n(j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j).\n\\]\n\nThese are another \\(n-1\\) rectangles; each one touches the bottom border of the board and extends up to the topmost row \\(n\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint (no cell belongs to both a horizontal and a vertical rectangle).  \nEvery unit square except the diagonal ones is covered, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell).  \nThe total number of rectangles used is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (4) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, while construction (5) achieves this number.  \nThus the bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Application to the given board  \n\nFor the \\(2025\\times2025\\) board (\\(n=2025\\)) the minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
